  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 219 AM EDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nHeading into Thursday, the mid-level western Atlantic ridge \ncontinues to build west towards the East Coast, while surface high \npressure remains in place over the Florida Peninsula. With deep \ntropical moisture in place (PWATs around 2.0-2.2 inches) and \npersistent light easterly flow, diurnal convection will be once \nagain largely focused over the interior and Southwest Florida. \nHowever, ample moisture will still support scattered morning and \nlate afternoon showers along the East Coast. Meanwhile, global and \nensemble guidance continues to depict a TUTT feature traversing \njust north of the Bahamas and approaching the Florida Peninsula \nThursday into Friday. By Friday, the overall synoptic picture will\nhinge on the influence of this disturbance as it tracks along the\nperiphery of the Bermuda high. Guidance remains split on its \nexact track and degree of influence over South Florida. One global\nensemble suite brings the disturbance closer to the peninsula, \nenhancing upper level support for convection, while another keeps \nthe TUTT further northeast off the Atlantic coast. Regardless of \nwhich scenario unfolds, the vast majority of ensemble members \ncluster around low- end QPF values (0.1 to 0.5 inches), suggesting\nthat widespread heavy accumulation is unlikely outside of any \nisolated outlier. \n\nHeading into Saturday and Sunday, the mid-level western Atlantic \nridge re-establishes its dominance over South Florida, maintaining a \nprevailing light to moderate southeasterly flow. A drier airmass \nwill filter into the region, with PWATs dropping into the 1.6-1.8 \ninch range, below climatological norms for this time of year. This \ndrier profile should act to further suppress widespread convective \nactivity. However, localized sea-breeze boundaries interacting with \nlingering low-level moisture could still trigger isolated to \nscattered afternoon showers and thunderstorms, primarily \nconcentrated over the interior and Southwest Florida. With the \nrobust ridge remaining entrenched, a warming trend will take hold \nthrough the weekend and persist into the beginning of next week. \nHighs will climb into the low 90s, just above seasonal norms, while \npeak heat index values reach 105-110 F. Heat impacts are expected to \nbecome an increasing concern, particularly across the East Coast \nmetro areas and Southwest Florida, where Moderate to Major HeatRisk \nis currently forecast on Sunday and Monday.",
